In this lesson we'll look at how to write up a statistical investigation properly, so your findings actually hold up. Think of a report as a short story about your data. First, state the question you investigated and the population it applies to. Then say where your sample came from, how big it was, and exactly how you picked those people, because random and convenience samples tell very different stories. Give your summary statistics with units, show a chart so the shape is visible, and write your conclusion in plain English. The bit students forget is uncertainty. Sampling variation means your sample mean probably won't exactly match the population mean, and a biased method can make even a confident-sounding number unreliable. So get into the habit of saying "based on this sample, we estimate" instead of declaring something as exact. When you make an inference about the population, keep it warranted by the data, bounded by the uncertainty, and ethical. Now let's see it in action.